WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.360
How do product, complement and

2
00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:05.100
reversal constructions work? Closure means

3
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.740
an operation applied to regular

4
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.120
languages produces another regular language.

5
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:10.880
We will construct the new

6
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.440
machines and explain what their

7
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.420
states mean, rather than memorize

8
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.760
a row of yes answers.

9
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.980
Let one machine track the

10
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.480
parity of zeros and another

11
00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:20.900
track the parity of ones.

12
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.060
The product machine stores both

13
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.640
current states as one ordered

14
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.760
pair. There are four possible

15
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.580
pairs, and both components update

16
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:29.820
on the very same input

17
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:30.200
symbol.

18
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.260
For union, mark a pair

19
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.060
accepting when either component is

20
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.700
accepting. For intersection, require both;

21
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.860
for difference, require the first

22
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.000
to accept and the second

23
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.060
to reject. The transitions need

24
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.600
not change, because the pair

25
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.600
already records enough information for

26
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.660
each Boolean condition.

27
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.680
The product invariant says that

28
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.820
after any prefix, each component

29
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.500
equals the state its original

30
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.260
machine would reach. It holds

31
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.580
before reading input and is

32
00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.640
preserved because each transition applies

33
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.120
both original transition functions. The

34
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.640
chosen accepting-pair rule therefore proves

35
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:04.980
both directions of the desired

36
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:05.780
language equality.

37
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.600
Complement is simpler, but its

38
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.780
hypothesis matters. Complete a deterministic

39
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.900
machine and replace its accepting

40
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.080
set by the other states,

41
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.580
relative to the same alphabet.

42
00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.640
Since every word has one

43
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.880
final state, exactly the previously

44
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.780
rejected words become accepted.

45
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.040
A missing transition breaks that

46
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.780
simple argument because a word

47
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.000
might have no full run

48
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.480
in the drawing. Add a

49
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.020
rejecting sink before complementing, so

50
00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.500
those words acquire a final

51
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.120
state too. For a nondeterministic

52
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.660
machine, merely flipping its accepting

53
00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.860
states is generally wrong because

54
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.120
acceptance is existential over branches.

55
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.380
For reversal, begin with a

56
00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.120
machine for the original language.

57
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.740
Reverse every arrow, make the

58
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.900
old start state accepting, and

59
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.140
add a fresh start with

60
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.940
epsilon arrows to every old

61
00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.280
accepting state. The result is

62
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.420
a nondeterministic machine because several

63
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.020
reversed arrows may share a

64
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:00.280
symbol.

65
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.460
An accepting original path reads

66
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.660
a word from start to

67
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.200
final. Walking that path backwards

68
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.380
in the new machine reads

69
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:09.620
the reversed word from an

70
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:10.860
old final to the old

71
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.220
start. Conversely, every new accepting

72
00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.940
path reverses back to an

73
00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.820
original accepting path, proving equality

74
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.040
in both directions.

75
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.560
For a tiny example, the

76
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.480
language containing only a followed

77
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.900
by b reverses to the

78
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.800
language containing only b followed

79
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.260
by a. The construction changes

80
00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.020
the path directions as well

81
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.180
as the start and final

82
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.840
roles. Reversing arrows without those

83
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.200
role changes would answer a

84
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:37.880
different question.

85
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.000
Things to take note of.

86
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.420
Products run both machines on

87
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.020
one shared word, complement requires

88
00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.840
a complete deterministic machine, and

89
00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.080
reversal uses nondeterministic starts. For

90
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.040
every closure proof, give the

91
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.080
construction and its invariant or

92
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.480
accepting-path correspondence, including the empty-word

93
00:02:58.480 --> 00:02:58.820
case.
